LOGBOOK.md:407:- ROOT CAUSE: r4 settled `macos.provider` with **no keychain row and no file exception** because `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb:792-793` grants `/Library/Keychains` read/write to any process holding `com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension`. Every observation was taken on **macOS 26.5 (25F71)**. The binding generated-target floor is **macOS 15.0** (`.spec/platform-distribution.md`, ADR-016/3r0993). A base sandbox grant is a property of the **running system**, not of this contract — so the matrix asserted, for nine major releases it never read, something true only of the one it did. Verdict 08 F1.
LOGBOOK.md:410:- FINDING: installed products settle it in **neither** direction. Re-read on this Mac: Surfshark ships the `/Library/Keychains/` exception at `LSMinimumSystemVersion` **10.15**, bundled with `/private/var/db/mds/` and a third path; PureVPN's two providers carry `keychain-access-groups` at **10.15**; Tailscale's carries neither at **12.4**. Support ranges that wide isolate nothing about macOS 15.0. K1's clause runs **both ways** — a key present in a shipping bundle is not proof the mechanism functions, and a key absent from one is not proof it is unnecessary.
LOGBOOK.md:412:- DECISION: rule **X1** gains a `conditionalExceptions` register — **reviewed but not granted** — holding pre-authorized entry **X1-C1** (`…temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-write` = `/Library/Keychains/`, `macos.provider`, both macOS channels, `armed: false`). It is **disjoint from the active register by construction**, because R26's equality (active register == the exceptions the rows actually author) is what keeps the active one honest. Rationale: the spec bans an *unreviewed* exception, so one a supported OS may require must be reviewed **before** the evidence — otherwise X1 rejects the right answer the day it arrives.
LOGBOOK.md:427:- FIX: one board edge — `link(TASK-260715-29ws8l, blocked_by=TASK-260715-ypo7yo)`, cycle-checked over the whole graph first. It orders all three because 29ws8l already blocks the other two, and it is semantically real: 29ws8l **AC2** specifies "App Group and `providerConfiguration` data" and **AC4** specifies "Keychain accessibility, access group" — exactly what rules G4 and K2 decide. Authoring it without this matrix reproduces the iOS-only assumption OC-1 was raised against.
LOGBOOK.md:443:- FINDING (**third instance, unreported by any verdict**): registering A6's scope required its clauses to render in the rationale document — which still carried the **r4** contract in its assertion table, granting `macos.host` the Keychain group **r5 removed under rule K2**. Identical to the verdict-05 F1 defect, one revision later and one file over. **R33 could not see it because R33 renders `grantClause`s, not assertions.** The table also had **no rows at all** for A17 and A18. Recorded in `S2.staleClaimsFound`; R35's rationale-document check closes that half.
LOGBOOK.md:469:- ROOT CAUSE: r5 moved eight target rows, two rules, two assertions and the C1 scope, and left `crossPlatformRules[2]` still asserting the **r4** contract — the shared Keychain access group granted to `macos.host`. The r5 validator passed **846 checks** because not one of its 31 rules looked at the summary. The generalisable defect is not the sentence: it is that a summary *asserting* a grant is a **second, unsynchronised statement** of data that lives elsewhere, and nothing in a per-row validator can see the contradiction. Reviewer verdict 05 F1.
LOGBOOK.md:474:- DECISION: the corrected Keychain sentence states the exclusion **universally** ("to NO macOS target on either channel, in either family") rather than naming the macOS host as excluded, which is a deliberate divergence from the reviewer's literal rework text. **Naming a target is what went stale**: r4 named the host as a grantee, r5 moved the row, the name stayed. A universal clause is derived from all eight rows and has nothing to forget. Recorded in the JSON as `whyTheMacOSHostIsNotNamed` rather than left as an unexplained difference.
LOGBOOK.md:481:- DECISION: `keychain-access-groups` is now **prohibited on all four macOS targets**. Rule **K2**: granted only where a **named second target** in the contract reads the same Data Protection Keychain item. Sharing is the entitlement's *only* function — it does not unlock the DPK, change durability, or survive anything the default group does not — so any argument for it that cannot name a second reader is an argument for something it does not do. On macOS there is no second reader: K1 excludes the root provider, and neither probe touches the DPK.
LOGBOOK.md:489:- SCOPE: `TASK-260728-q5kjta` (Ceremony C1) scope + AC4 corrected on the board (verdict 04 F2): four macOS App IDs not a singular pair, Network Extensions only, **zero** App Group records/capabilities, **no** Keychain mutation (there is no portal record), Mac Development profile class, all four iOS App IDs excluded. A downstream-consequence paragraph in the matrix is not the contract the operator reads.
LOGBOOK.md:507:- FINDING: `kSecUseKeychain` is **add-only**. `SecItem.h` defines it as "a value of type `SecKeychainRef` to which **`SecItemAdd`** will add the provided item(s)"; scoping a *search* takes `kSecMatchSearchList`. r1 specified `kSecUseKeychain` for the query. Verified end to end against a throwaway keychain — add/read/delete all `OSStatus=0`, and a **negative control** with the search list pointed at the user default returns `-25300 errSecItemNotFound`, proving the list actually scopes rather than being silently ignored. This also gives `379cpk`/`1o9wjz` a real unit-test seam needing neither root nor the System keychain.
LOGBOOK.md:508:- FINDING: Do not hard-code `/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`. `SecKeychainCopyDomainDefault(kSecPreferencesDomainSystem, &kc)` returns `OSStatus=0` and resolves to exactly that path from an ordinary user context (uid 502), and `SecKeychainCopyDomainSearchList` for the system domain has **count=1** — physically confirming TN3137's *"in the system context the search list includes just the System keychain"*. The fix is behaviour-preserving, so there was never a reason to pin the literal. The whole `SecKeychain`/`SecAccess` surface is `API_DEPRECATED(… macos(10.2, 10.10))` yet still ships in the 26.5 SDK and remains Apple's only documented daemon option.
LOGBOOK.md:509:- ANOMALY: `Security.framework/Headers/SecKeychain.h` is **ISO-8859 encoded**. A plain UTF-8 `grep` over it silently prints nothing and exits 1 — indistinguishable from "the symbol isn't there". This nearly produced the opposite error, concluding the domain APIs had been removed from the SDK. `iconv -f ISO-8859-1 -t UTF-8` first when grepping Apple headers; an empty grep result on a header is not evidence of absence.
LOGBOOK.md:513:- DECISION: Candidate D selected. Host reads the secret in user context, hands it to the running provider via `sendProviderMessage` → `handleAppMessage`, and the **root provider** persists it in the file-based **System keychain** (`/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`) with a restrictive `SecAccess`. This is exactly Apple DTS's recommendation for this shape (forums/thread/759976, /133933). Rejected: shared Data Protection Keychain group (cannot work), memory-only IPC seed (dies on system start), custom XPC Mach service (needs exception entitlements + makes OC-4 live), root-owned encrypted file (Tailscale's pattern — same security, more code, more entitlements).
LOGBOOK.md:514:- FINDING: The decisive fact is physically readable on this Mac. `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb:792-793` (macOS 26.5/25F71, sha256 `2e711078…`): `(when (entitlement "com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension") (allow file-read* file-write* (subpath "/Library/Keychains")))`. An NE-entitled process already has System keychain access from the base App Sandbox. Lines 655-656 grant the `securityd`/`SecurityServer` Mach lookups to every sandboxed process. **The selected transport costs zero entitlements.**
LOGBOOK.md:515:- DECISION: `keychain-access-groups` on `works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel` — **keep prohibited**, promoted from `prohibited-pending-decision` to settled. Not "unnecessary" but *inapplicable*: the file-based keychain is ACL-controlled (`SecAccess`), access groups are a Data Protection Keychain concept only (TN3137). Applying owner is `TASK-260715-ypo7yo`; closes OC-1, downgrades OC-4.
LOGBOOK.md:516:- ANOMALY: Second instance of the pattern logged at 0755 — **shipping entitlements are not evidence of requirement**. Surfshark's provider ships a `/Library/Keychains/` temporary exception the current sandbox profile makes redundant; Surfshark and Tailscale both ship `/private/var/db/mds/` exceptions dating to a Catalina-era bug fixed in Big Sur (forums/thread/660123), which `application.sb:544-546` already covers for uid 0; and PureVPN's provider carries `keychain-access-groups` that Facts 1-2 say cannot bridge the user boundary. Three shipping products, three stale or inert entitlements.
LOGBOOK.md:517:- FINDING: **The App Group channel is broken on macOS too**, not just the keychain. `containerURL(forSecurityApplicationGroupIdentifier:)` resolves relative to the caller's home and the root provider's home is `/private/var/root` (verified root-private, `ls` exit 1). `.spec/architecture.md:111` says shared state is "an App Group configuration snapshot and Keychain references" — both halves fail on macOS. The non-secret snapshot must travel in `providerConfiguration`, which the accepted M1 lifecycle contract already does. Directly affects `TASK-260715-3f4lxy`.
LOGBOOK.md:519:- FINDING: System keychain **attributes are world-readable** — `security dump-keychain /Library/Keychains/System.keychain` as uid 502 exits 0 and prints 192 items' attributes. Item *data* is ACL-gated (`-d` blocked on an interactive authorization prompt, killed at 120s). Consequence: the credential item's service/account values must carry no hostname, account, or profile name.
LOGBOOK.md:520:- NOTE: Experiment design worth reusing. `ssh localhost` gives a **`Background`** launchd session at the same uid while the terminal is **`Aqua`** — a free, no-root way to isolate the "user login context" axis. It showed the file-based login keychain returning `OSStatus=0` in Aqua and `-25308 errSecInteractionNotAllowed` in Background. It could **not** test the Data Protection Keychain: an ad-hoc-signed CLI returns `-34018 errSecMissingEntitlement` in both contexts, because DP access groups must be authorized by a provisioning profile (TN3137) and Ceremony C1 has not run.
LOGBOOK.md:521:- BLOCKED: Nothing. Named limitation instead — `sudo -n true` exit 1 in a non-interactive session, so **no code was run as root**. Checks V1–V3 (V1 System keychain write from the signed sandboxed provider, V2 root group-container path, V3 system start with the app not running) were appended to `TASK-260715-9yp8to` rather than creating a duplicate physical-Mac node (ADR-028a). r2 added **V4/V5** to the same node.
LOGBOOK.md:534:- DECISION: Every NE value in the contract is now keyed by channel (`development` / `developer-id` / `app-store`), each profile row records `authorizesNetworkExtensionValues`, and the C1 scope is pinned to development-channel values so the ceremony cannot be pointed at the wrong one. Consequence worth remembering: the probe family is development-signed only, so it can prove the **development** entitlement shape and nothing else — r1 implied it proved the production shape.
LOGBOOK.md:544:- ANOMALY: Every Apple example of an app group used as a sandboxed **Mach service name prefix**, and all three shipping products inspected here, use the macOS-style Team-ID form — and all predate Feb 2025. No source found confirms an iOS-style ID works as a Mach service prefix. Not blocking: the spec's host↔provider channel is an App Group snapshot plus Keychain references (`.spec/architecture.md:111`), not XPC. Recorded as **OC-4** owned by `TASK-260728-7ii1xz`, with the amendment path being a separate *unregistered* macOS-style ID for that one purpose. This supersedes the 0657 claim below that the macOS group is required "as the XPC Mach-service namespace" — that purpose is now conditional, not established.
LOGBOOK.md:545:- FINDING: The r1 D-2 rationale ("the entitlement would be non-functional") was too strong. PureVPN's root sysexes carry `keychain-access-groups` outright and Surfshark's carries a `/Library/Keychains/` path exception — i.e. the **file-based System keychain** works from root. What fails is specifically the *Data Protection* Keychain sharing the spec asks for. The row stays withheld because its purpose is undetermined until `7ii1xz` picks a transport, not because it is inert.
LOGBOOK.md:553:### 0657 — The macOS provider runs as root, so the specified Keychain sharing cannot work (TASK-260715-ypo7yo)
LOGBOOK.md:554:- BLOCKER: `.spec/security-privacy.md` requires private keys and passphrases to live in the Data Protection Keychain "shared only through the minimum keychain access group required by the app and its packet tunnel extension" (also `.spec/threat-model.md` DF-02/M-02). On macOS that is unsatisfiable. Developer ID direct distribution forces the provider to be a **system extension** (TN3134, `.spec/platform-distribution.md`), a sysex runs **as root** outside a user context, the Data Protection Keychain is available only to code in a user context, and keychain access groups share items between programs running as the **same** user. The host runs as the logged-in user. iOS is unaffected — its appex runs as the same user as its host.
